---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Handicap Placard Rules 2026: Who Publishes This Site';
const description = 'Handicap Placard By State in 2026: who publishes this guide to the placard rules of the 50 states and DC, the official forms behind it, and what it never sells.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Handicap Placard By State</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this placard guide exists</h2>
    <p>Someone whose placard is about to expire, or who just left the hospital with a temporary need, asks plain questions: which form, what it costs, who can sign it, and whether the doctor must sign again. The answers are scattered over {K.C.total} agency sites, each with its own words, and the pages that rank first often sell a paid medical evaluation.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators and guides on public rules. It is an independent site, not a government site and not a DMV. It sells nothing, files no application, takes no commission, carries no affiliate link and asks for no contact details.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What it relies on</h2>
    <p>The disability parking application forms and pages of the motor vehicle agencies of the 50 states and the District of Columbia, and the federal Uniform System for Parking for Persons with Disabilities, 23 CFR part 1235, read on eCFR. All were read on {K.day(K.READ)}.</p>
    <h2>What it leaves out</h2>
    <p>Medical questions of any kind and whether you qualify; license plates for people with disabilities; placards for organizations; local parking rules. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every limit and the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for a form that changed or a rule we read wrong. We answer questions about a form or a rule, not about your own application, which only your state agency can decide.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
